      "text": "The root cause is that `SparsePauliOp` expects Pauli labels or Pauli objects, not a numeric matrix. The old `MatrixOp(...).to_pauli_op()` decomposed the matrix into Paulis, so the equivalent is to build an `Operator` from the matrix and then call `SparsePauliOp.from_operator`.\n\n```python\n# FIX: SparsePauliOp was not imported alongside Operator -> added Operator to the import, because the matrix must first be wrapped in an Operator before it can be decomposed into Paulis\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp, Operator\n\nHamiltonian = [\n    [ 4.07981221, -3.6713615 , 1.3943662 , -1.05164319],\n    [-3.6713615 , 5.88262911, -4.14084507, 1.37558685],\n    [ 1.3943662 , -4.14084507, 5.83098592, -3.54929577],\n    [-1.05164319, 1.37558685, -3.54929577, 3.79812207]\n]\n\n# FIX: SparsePauliOp(Hamiltonian).to_operator() passed a numeric matrix to SparsePauliOp, which only accepts Pauli labels -> wrapped the matrix in Operator and used SparsePauliOp.from_operator to decompose it into Pauli terms, because this matches the old MatrixOp.to_pauli_op() behaviour\nH_op = SparsePauliOp.from_operator(Operator(Hamiltonian))\nprint(H_op)\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
